s sum 7 >6, so not in T_5. So only (1,2),(2,3) are in T_5, that's 2 points. So not enough. y = x-1 includes (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) sum 7 >6, so only (2,1),(3,2) are in T_5. So not enough. So slope 1 line y=x is the only one with 3 points.\n\nNow slope -2 lines: y = -2x + c. For integer points, we need c such that points are positive and sum <=6. Let's find lines with 3 points. For slope -2, the sum x+y = x + (-2x + c) = -x + c. So sum decreases by 1 each step (since p+q = -2+1 = -1? Wait p = -2, q = 1 => p+q = -1, absolute 1. Actually slope -2 corresponds to p = -2, q = 1, p+q = -1, absolute 1. So bound is floor((k+1)/2) = 3. So can have up to 3 points. Let's find a line with 3 points. For p=-2,q=1, line equation -2x - y = c => y = -2x - c. Actually we need to be careful: The general integer line is p x - q y = c. So with p=-2, q=1, we have -2x - y = c => y = -2x - c. So slope -2, intercept -c. For integer points, we need y positive. Let's set c = -5 => y = -2x +5. Then points: x=1 => y=3 (sum 4), x=2 => y=1 (sum 3), x=3 => y=-1 (invalid). So only two points. For c = -4 => y = -2x +4: x=1 => y=2 (sum 3), x=2 => y=0 (invalid). So only one point. For c = -6 => y = -2x +6: x=1 => y=4 (sum 5), x=2 => y=2 (sum 4), x=3 => y=0 (invalid). So two points. For c = -7 => y = -2x +7: x=1 => y=5 (sum 6), x=2 => y=3 (sum 5), x=3 => y=1 (sum 4), x=4 => y=-1 (invalid). So three points: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). All sums <=6. So line y = -2x +7 works, covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). That's 3 points. Good.\n\nNow slope -1/2 lines: p = -1, q = 2 => p+q = 1, absolute 1. Equation -x - 2y = c? Wait p x - q y = c => -1*x - 2*y = c => y = -(x + c)/2. Actually slope = p/q = -1/2. Let's find lines with 3 points. For p=-1,q=2, line: -x - 2y = c => 2y = -x - c => y = -(x + c)/2. For integer y, x + c must be even. Let's try c = -5 => y = -(x -5)/2 = (5 - x)/2. For x=1 => y=2 (sum 3), x=3 => y=1 (sum 4), x=5 => y=0 (invalid). So two points. For c = -7 => y = -(x -7)/2 = (7 - x)/2. x=1 => y=3 (sum 4), x=3 => y=2 (sum 5), x=5 => y=1 (sum 6), x=7 => y=0 (invalid). So three points: (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). All sums <=6. So line y = -(x -7)/2 works.\n\nThus we have three lines: y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3)), y = -2x +7 (covers (1,5),(2,3),(3,1)), y = -(x -7)/2 (covers (1,3),(3,2),(5,1)). That's 9 points. We need 15 points. So we need two more lines each covering 3 points. Let's see if we can find other lines with 3 points. Perhaps slope 2 lines? Let's test slope 2 (p=2,q=1 => p+q=3). Bound floor((k+1)/2) = 3, but with |p+q|=3, the bound is floor((k-1)/3)+1 = floor(4/3)+1 = 1+1 = 2. So slope 2 lines can have at most 2 points. So not enough.\n\nWhat about slope -3 (p=-3,q=1 => p+q=-2, absolute 2). That could have up to 3 points. Let's test line y = -3x + c. For c = 8 => y = -3x +8: x=1 => y=5 (sum 6), x=2 => y=2 (sum 4), x=3 => y=-1 (invalid). So two points. For c = 9 => y = -3x +9: x=1 => y=6 (sum 7 >6 invalid). So not. For c = 7 => y = -3x +7: x=1 => y=4 (sum 5), x=2 => y=1 (sum 3), x=3 => y=-2 invalid. So two points. So slope -3 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope 1/2 (p=1,q=2 => p+q=3). Bound 2 points. So not enough.\n\nThus only lines with |p+q|=1 or 2 can have up to 3 points. For |p+q|=1, we have p+q = ±1. That includes many possibilities: p=1,q=0? No q>0. So p+q=1 => p = 1 - q. Since q>0, p can be 0 (if q=1) but p=0 is horizontal, not allowed. So q=2 => p=-1 (p+q=1). That's p=-1,q=2, which we already have (slope -1/2). q=3 => p=-2 (p+q=1). That's p=-2,q=3 => slope -2/3. Let's test if that line can have 3 points. For p=-2,q=3, p+q=1, bound floor((k+1)/2)=3. So possible. Let's test line -2x - 3y = c => y = -(2x + c)/3. For integer y, 2x + c must be divisible by 3. Let's try to find a line with 3 points in T_5. Choose c such that we get points (x,y) positive and sum <=6. Let's try c = -5 => y = -(2x -5)/3 = (5 - 2x)/3. For x=1 => y = (5-2)/3 = 1 (sum 2). For x=2 => y = (5-4)/3 = 1/3 not integer. For x=3 => y = (5-6)/3 = -1/3 not integer. So only one point.