sunny), L3 (non-sunny). The non-sunny line can be vertical (x = constant), horizontal (y = constant), or diagonal (x+y = constant). Let's consider each case.\n\nCase 1: Non-sunny line is vertical x = n-2 (covers the three points in column n-2). Then we need to cover the remaining points (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1) with two sunny lines. As we saw earlier, any line covering any pair among these three points is either horizontal (n-1,1)-(n,1) => y=1, not sunny; vertical (n-1,1)-(n-1,2) => x=n-1, not sunny; diagonal (n-1,2)-(n,1) => slope -1, not sunny. So we cannot cover any pair with a sunny line. So we would need each point to be covered by a separate sunny line, requiring three sunny lines, not allowed. So vertical x=n-2 is not possible.\n\nCase 2: Non-sunny line is vertical x = n-1 (covers (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)). Then we need to cover (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n,1) with two sunny lines. That's four points. Could we cover them with two sunny lines? Possibly. Let's see.\n\nWe have points in column n-2: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). And point (n,1). We need two sunny lines covering these four points. Let's try to find a sunny line covering (n-2,3) and (n,1). Slope = (1-3)/(n-(n-2)) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny. So not allowed. (n-2,3) and (n-2,1) vertical, not sunny. (n-2,3) and (n-2,2) vertical, not sunny. So any line covering (n-2,3) with any other point among these four is either vertical (if with (n-2,1) or (n-2,2)) or slope -1 (if with (n,1)). So (n-2,3) cannot be covered by a sunny line with any of the other points. So we would need a separate sunny line for (n-2,3). That leaves three points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n,1) to be covered by one sunny line. Let's see if any line covering two of them is sunny. (n-2,1) and (n-2,2) vertical, not sunny. (n-2,1) and (n,1) horizontal, not sunny. (n-2,2) and (n,1) slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-2)) = -1/2, which is not 0, infinite, or -1. So that line is sunny! So we could have L1: line through (n-2,2) and (n,1) slope -1/2, sunny. That covers those two